{"id":"s1.3.4","slug":"sum-of-squares-factoring-method","title":"Sum of Squares: Factoring Method","content":"<p class=\"mb-4\">Problems that look like $(a \\pm b)^2 + (c \\pm d)^2$ can often be simplified by expanding the terms using FOIL. This avoids direct, cumbersome calculations. Usually, the numbers involved (like $a, b, c, d$) are chosen to be simple, such as multiples of 10 or 5.</p><p class=\"mb-4\">For example, instead of calculating $19^2 + (10^2 - 9^2)$, you can recognize that $19 = 10+9$ and $10^2 - 9^2 = (10-9)(10+9)=19$. So the problem is $19^2+19$. An even better way is to expand $(10+9)^2 + (10^2-9^2)$, although this specific example in the text seems to have a typo. The general idea is to use algebraic identities to your advantage, such as $(a+b)^2 = a^2+2ab+b^2$ and difference of squares, to break down the problem into more manageable pieces.</p>","summary":"Problems that look like can often be simplified by expanding the terms using FOIL.","generation_guideline":"Generate a problem involving sums and differences of squares, like `(a+b)^2 + (a^2-b^2)`.","questions":[{"id":1,"text":"Calculate $(11 + 10)^2 + (11^2 - 10^2)$.","answer":"462","kind":"exact","tags":["arithmetic"]},{"id":2,"text":"Calculate $(30 + 2)^2 + (30^2 - 2^2)$.","answer":"1920","kind":"exact","tags":["arithmetic"]},{"id":3,"text":"Calculate $(10 + 9)^2 + (10^2 - 9^2)$.","answer":"380","kind":"exact","tags":["arithmetic"]},{"id":4,"text":"Calculate $(30 + 2)^2 - (30^2 - 2^2)$.","answer":"128","kind":"exact","tags":["arithmetic"]},{"id":5,"text":"Calculate $24^2 - (20^2 + 4^2)$.","answer":"160","kind":"exact","tags":["arithmetic"]},{"id":6,"text":"Calculate $31^2 - (29^2 - 2^2)$.","answer":"124","kind":"exact","tags":["arithmetic"]},{"id":7,"text":"Calculate $(30^2 - 2^2) + (30 + 2)^2$.","answer":"1920","kind":"exact","tags":["arithmetic"]},{"id":8,"text":"Calculate $81^2 + (80 + 1)(80 - 1)$.","answer":"12960","kind":"exact","tags":["arithmetic"]},{"id":9,"text":"Calculate $55^2 - 